.ds CH INTRODUCTION
.he //\\*CH/MROFF USER'S MANUAL/
.fo //-%-//
..
.sp 5
Introduction
.sp
MROFF is one of a family of three text processors geared to
suit any need.
The family is SROFF (small), MROFF (medium), and LROFF (large).
They differ in capability, size, and complexity, ranging from
baby to papa.
They accept lines of text interspersed with lines of format control
information and format the text into a printable, paginated document
having a user defined style.
They offer unusual freedom in document styling, including arbitrary
style headers and footers, paragraph numbering, control over page
boundaries, boldface and underline.
The family syntax is compatible, although commands available in the
most complex are not available in the least complex.
.sp 2
Usage
.sp
The general form of invoking MROFF at the OS9 command level is
.sp
.ti +10
mroff options files
.sp
where options represent any of a number of option arguments and files
represents the list of files containing the document to be formatted.
Options and files are processed in the order in which they are found.
An argument starting with a minus (-) is taken to be an option.
The options available are:
.sp 2
.in +10
.ti -8
Option\ \ Effect
.sp
.ti -8
-pN\ \ \ \ \ Use N as the page offset, moving all output to the
right by N spaces.  Has no effect on margins.
.sp
.ti -8
-oN-M\ \ \ Print only pages N through M.  If M is missing, N
through the end will be printed.  If N is missing, 1 through M will
be printed.
.sp
.ti -8
-b\ \ \ \ \ \ The default mode for bold and underline is to use a
character, backspace, character sequence.  This works best for
terminals and will work with a printer that supports backspacing.
For other cases this option causes the return and line feed functions
to be separated to permit restrike to handle bold and underline.
.in
.sp 2
.ne 10
This manual is divided into three parts.
The first is an index and command summary.
Each section title is followed by the commands discussed in that
section.
The commands are also listed in alphabetic order with the section
where they are discussed.
The second section is a reference manual.
It is keyed numerically to the index and cross reference.
The third section is examples of use, also keyed to the index.
The hope is that the numeric keys will help to find material
quickly in this manual.
.sp
NOTE: Some minor changes were made to the original MROFF code to allow
some compatibility with UNIX NROFF.  These changes are noted in the
command summary.  In all cases, the UNIX macro functions exactly the
same as the MROFF equivalent. (Mark Griffith)
.ds CH "SUMMARY AND INDEX"
.pg
.nf
\&Request Initial If No
\&Form     Value  Argument Notes Explanation

\&1. General Explanation

\&2. Page Control
\&.ne N     -     1        -     Need N vertical spaces.
\&.pg +/-N  1     -        -     Eject current page; next page #N.
\&.bp +/-N  1     -        -     Same as above - UNIX compatibility
\&.pl +/-N  66    66       B     Page length.
\&.po +/-N  0     previous -     Page offset.

\&3. Text Filling, Adjusting, and Centering
\&.br       -     -        B     Break the current output line.
\&.ce N     off   1        B     Center the following N lines.
\&.fi       fill  -        B     Fill output lines.
\&.ju       just  -        -     Adjust line for even margins.
\&.nf       fill  -        B     No fill or justify of output.
\&.nj       just  -        -     No output adjustment.

\&4. Vertical Spacing
\&.ls N     1     previous -     Output N-1 blank lines per line.
\&.sp N     -     1        B     Space vertically N lines.

\&5. Line Length and Indenting
\&.in +/-N  0     previous B     Indent from page edge.
\&.rm +/-N  72    previous -     Right margin.
\&.ti +/-N  -     ignored  B     Temporary indent.

\&6. Three Part Titles
\&.ef /left/center/right/  -     Footer for even pages.
\&.eh /left/center/right/  -     Header for even pages.
\&.fo /left/center/right/  -     Footer for all pages.
\&.he /left/center/right/  -     Header for all pages.
\&.tl /left/center/right/  -     Same as above - UNIX compatibility
\&.lt +/-N  72    previous -     Set title length
\&.m1 +/-N  2     2        -     Set margin  1
\&.m2 +/-N  2     2        -     Set margin  2
\&.m3 +/-N  2     2        -     Set margin  3
\&.m4 +/-N  2     2        -     Set margin  4
\&.oh /left/center/right/  -     Header for odd pages.
\&.of /left/center/right/  -     Footer for odd pages.
\&.pc c     #     #        -     Page number character.

\&7. Macros and Strings
\&.dm xx    -     error    -     Define macro xx.
\&.de xx    -     error    -     Same as above - UNIX compatibility
\&.ds xx string   ignored  -     Define xx to contain "string".
\&..        -     -        -     End macro definition (also .em, .en).

\&8. Number Registers
\&.nr R +/-N      -        -     Define and set number register R.

\&9. Output and Input Conventions and Conversions
\&.bo N     off   1        -     Print bold N input lines.
\&.cc c     .     .        -     Set command character to c.
\&.cu N     off   1        -     Continuously underline.
\&.ec c     \\     \\        -     Set the escape character.
\&.ul N     off   1        -     Underline N input lines.
.pg
\&Request Initial If No
\&Form     Value  Argument Notes Explanation

\&10. Input File Switching
\&.so name  -     -        -     Switch to file "name".

\&11. Miscellaneous
\&.*                             Comment (also ..)
\&.bs N     1     1        -     Use full line overstrike.

\&12. Error Messages

Notes - B means request causes a break.
.fi
