.ds CH "REFERENCE MANUAL"
.pg
.MH "General Explanation"
.mh "Form of input."
Input consists of text lines, which are destined
to be printed, interspersed with control line, which set parameters
or otherwise control subsequent processing. Control lines begin with
a control character (normally .) followed by a two character name
that specifies a basic request or the substitution of a user defined
macro in place of the control line. The request/macro name may be
separated from the control character by spaces, and must be followed
by either space or a return. Control lines with unrecognized names
are reported as errors.
.pp
Various special functions may be introduced anywhere in the input by
means of an escape character (normally \\). For example, \\nR causes
the contents of the number register R to be inserted instead.
.pp
It may prove useful to understand how MROFF process input text.
MROFF reads a line of text from the source file, expanding any
escape sequences that it finds in the line.
If the first character of the line is the command character,
the macro table is searched for a macro with a name that
matches the two following characters.
If one is found, it is read as if it were from the source file.
Any escape sequences found are expanded,
and processing continues with this new line of text.
That is, it is searched for a macro or command etc.
If a macro was not found, the internal command table is searched for
a command that matches the following two characters.
If one is found, it is executed.
If none is found, the error is reported.
If the first character of the line is not the command character,
the line is processed as text, either output directly if not
in the fill mode, or added to the current output line if in the
fill mode.
.mh "Formatter and device resolution."
This version supports only
whole line and whole character spacing.  All requests that accept
spacing parameters expect them to be in the appropriate units, either
lines or characters.
.mh "Numerical expressions."
This version does not support expressions
as arguments for requests or macros, although in most cases a number
register can be used as an argument.
.mh "Notation."
Numerical parameters are indicated in this manual in
two ways. +/-N means that the argument can take the forms of N, +N,
or -N and that the corresponding effect is to set the affected
parameter to N, increment it by N, or decrement it by N,
respectively. Plain N means that the parameter can only be set to N.
Generally, unreasonable numerical input is either ignored or
truncated to a reasonable value.  Most requests expect to set
parameters to non-negative values; .pg being the only exception. The
requests .po, .ls, .rm, .in, .lt, .m1, .m2, .m3, and .m4 restore the previous
parameter value in the absence of a parameter, while other requests set
the parameter to the default value.
Single character arguments are indicated by a single lower case
letter and two character arguments are indicated by a pair of lower
case letters. Character string arguments are indicated by
multi-character mnemonics.
.MH "Page Control"
.mh "Margins."
Top and bottom margins are automatically provided.
The .m1, .m2, .m3, and .m4 requests are used to control the top and
bottom margins.
See section 6.2 for details.
The .pg request is used to force a new page,
optionally with an different number than the next one in sequence.
The .pn request will reset the current page number to a different
value without causing a page eject.
.mh "Boundaries."
The .pl request is used to set the page length.
The .po request is used to shift all output text to the right to give
a binding margin.  It has no affect on any of the other parameters.
.mh "White Space."
The .ne request tells the processor that the user wants at least N
vertical spaces to print the following text.
If less is available, a new page will be started.
This is used to keep together lines that should not be split across a
page boundary.
.MH "Text Filling, Adjusting, and Centering"
.mh "Filling."
Normally words are collected from input
text lines and assembled into an output text line until something
doesn't fit.
A word is any string of characters that is delimited
by spaces or the beginning/end of the input line. Any adjacent pair
of words that must be kept together (neither split across a line nor
spread apart in the adjustment process) can be tied together by
separating them with the unpaddable space character "\\ "
(escape character followed by a space).
.pp
Any input text line ending with ., ?, or ! is taken to be the end of
a sentence and an additional space character is provided during
filling. Leading spaces are retained, and will cause a break, but
multiple inter-word spaces are not retained unless made unpaddable.
.pp
The .br request causes a break. This means that the filling of the
current output line is stopped and the line is output without
adjustment. Text lines beginning with a space and blank lines also
cause a break.
.pp
The .fi request starts fill on subsequent lines and the .nf request
stops filling.  In the no-fill mode output lines are not adjusted;
they are output without regard for the current line length. Both
requests cause a break.
.mh "Adjusting."
The .ju request will start right margin adjustment. If fill is not on,
adjustment will not start until the fill mode is entered.
The spaces between the words on the output line are then
increased to spread out the line to the current line length minus any
current indent.
The adjusted word spacing is not uniform because
of the granularity of the output device, but alternate lines are
padded from alternate ends to minimize the effects.
The .nj request turns adjustment off; the right margin will be ragged.
This version does not support proportional spacing printers.
To do so requires knowledge of the algorithms used by the printer.
.mh "Centering."
The .ce request will center the next N lines within the current text
window (right margin minus indent). If N is 0, any residual count is
cleared and centering stops.  A break occurs after each of the N
lines and no other adjustment is done to the line.  A line wider than
the current text window will end up left justified.
.MH "Vertical Spacing"
.mh "Vertical Spacing."
A vertical block of spaces is ordinarily requested using the .sp request.
MROFF will not space past the bottom of a page.
.pp
The .ne request (section 2.3) is used to reserve a
block of space. The .ls request is used to set the line spacing. One less
than the argument blank lines will separate lines of text.  For example
an argument of one sets single spacing, with no blank lines, while an
argument of two sets double spacing, with one blank line between text
lines.
.MH "Line Length and Indenting"
.mh "Line Length and Indenting."
.br
The right margin (maximum line length) for the fill mode may be set
with the .rm request.
The indent may be set with the .in request.
An indent applicable to only the next output line may be set with
the .ti request.
The line length includes indent space but not page offset space
(section 2.2).
The line length minus the indent is the basis for centering with
the .ce request.
The effect of .rm or .ti is delayed, if a partially collected line exists,
until that line is output. The use of .in will cause a break, forcing
the output of any partially filled line.
Note that .rm has no control in the non fill mode, although indenting
still works.
.MH "Three Part Titles"
.mh "Titles."
Three part titles defined with the .he request would be used on all
pages.  Using the .eh and .oh requests will cause one to appear on
even numbered pages and the other on odd numbered pages.
Similarly, the .fo request defines a three part footer.
Using the .ef and .of requests define even and odd page footers.
If the page character (initially %) is found in a title or footer
string, it will be replaced with the current internal page number.
The .pc request can be used to change the page character from the
default (%) to something else.
The .lt request sets the width of the title and footer lines,
independently of the margins.
.mh "Margins."
The .m1 and .m2 requests set the margin sizes at the top
of the page, while the .m3 and .m4 requests set the margin
sizes at the bottom of the page.
The header will be placed in the last line of the m1 space, with
one less line than m1 above it.
This will be followed by m2 blank lines before the body of the
text starts.
When m3 plus m4 lines are left on the page, MROFF stops
printing text and produces m3 blank lines.
The footer will be placed in the first line of the m4 space
with one less line than m4 below.
Setting all four to zero will suppress all paging, while setting
m1 and m4 to zero will disable titles and footers, respectively,
even if header and footer strings are defined.
.MH "Macros and Strings"
.mh "Macros and Strings."
A macro is a named set of lines that may be invoked by name. A string
is a named string of characters that may be introduced into the input
stream at any point by using its name. Macro and string names are two
character names that may usurp previously defined request, macro or
string names. Macros are created with the .dm request and may be
redefined but not removed.
The .. request is used to terminate a macro.
A macro is invoked in the same way as a request; a control line
beginning with .xx will inject the contents of the macro xx into the
processing stream.
The remainder of the line may contain up to 10 arguments, numbered 0
through 9.
Macros can be used in macros, but nesting too deep or recursion
will cause unpredictable results.
.pp
The .ds request is used to create a string.
The string following the request is saved for substitution at
a later time.
Note that the string cannot be continued onto other lines.
The st